  Have you had the chance to explain what you do to someone else?
  Someone who doesn't know anything about typography, or who doesn’t
  speak the same language as you? Or\Dash if English is your mother
  tongue\Dash to someone who is not as fluent? To do any of these, there are
  many criteria to be taken into consideration if you want a
  meaningful exchange, here in the context of typography, but in fact,
  isn’t it the same for everything in life?
  
  First published in January 2025 at the event of Face/Interface
  conference at Stanford University, Words of Type (\url{wordsoftype.com})
  brings together the
  terms used in typography and typeface design in a multilingual and
  online encyclopedia. Its first version contains 200 terms,
  illustrated and explained into seven languages, and aims to grow
  with additional words and languages.

  The goals of the project are: first, to bring easier access to
  typography knowledge; then, to nurture diversity in the field, from
  the smaller worlds of various expertises related to it, to those of
  the people speaking the various languages of the larger world, and
  to make our lives as typographers ever more exciting.
